@page "/scroll-focus"

<PageTitle>Scroll &amp; focus</PageTitle>
<h1>Scroll &amp; focus — click-to-focus across a long form</h1>
<p>Twelve teams of three members, all static — this page is about distance, not editing. Submit, then click any entry in the summary: the page scrolls the element into view, centred, and focuses it.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "ScrollFocus.razor", "ScrollFocus.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li>A blocked submit auto-focuses the first error by default (<code>FormidableForm.FocusFirstErrorOnInvalidSubmit</code>) — untick the toggle above the form to turn that off and choose focus yourself instead.</li>
            <li>The summary lists every visible issue. Clicking an entry scrolls its element into view (centred) and focuses it.</li>
            <li>The focus service reports whether the element took focus, not merely whether it found one — and every target on this page is an ordinary input that is rendered and willing, so every entry works with no fallback.</li>
            <li>Contrast the Virtualized page: its rows leave the DOM when scrolled out of the render window, so it needs a <code>FocusFallback</code> to bring one back before focusing it. Here there is nothing to bring back.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Submit — the summary appears and focus jumps straight to the first error, no click needed.</li>
            <li>Click the LAST entry in the summary — the page scrolls to the bottom row and focuses it.</li>
            <li>Fix it, submit, click another far entry — same ride back.</li>
            <li>Untick the toggle above the form and submit again — this time focus stays put; use the summary to get there yourself.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<p>
    <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_focusFirstError" /> Focus the first error automatically on a blocked submit</label>
</p>

<FormidableForm Model="_roster" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid" FocusFirstErrorOnInvalidSubmit="@_focusFirstError">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>
    <FormidableCollectionMessage For="() => _roster.Teams" />

    @foreach (var team in _roster.Teams)
    {
        <fieldset @key="team">
            <legend>Team</legend>
            <div class="field"><label>Name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="team.Name" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => team.Name" /></div>

            <FormidableCollectionMessage For="() => team.Members" />
            <ul class="member-list">
                @foreach (var member in team.Members)
                {
                    <li class="field" @key="member">
                        <label>Alias <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="member.Alias" /></label>
                        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => member.Alias" />
                    </li>
                }
            </ul>
        </fieldset>
    }

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
